--- docs/sota_sprint/novelty_audit.md ---
     1	# SOTA sprint novelty audit (Agent F)
     2	
     3	2026-11-03. Scope: the mechanisms behind the 24 candidates in `candidates.json` (families A to D),
     4	for the contract T=2000, B=200 paid delayed labels, switching cost, anytime false-promotion
     5	control (see `docs/failure_atlas_v2_flq/results/REPORT.md`, M1 to M4).
     6	
     7	How the citations were checked: each one was looked up on 2026-11-03 through arXiv abstract pages,
     8	publisher or PMLR pages, or a web search that returned the publisher record. Entries marked
     9	**UNVERIFIED** were not confirmed. This is a literature audit only. Nothing here is an empirical
    10	result. "No prior art found" means our searches returned nothing. It does not show that nothing
    11	exists.
    12	
    13	## Cross-cutting finding (read first)
    14	
    15	There is already a strong, assumption-light answer to "is the challenger better, valid under
    16	arbitrary dependence". It is the weak-null, time-averaged conditional mean of paired score
    17	differences, from Choe & Ramdas (*Comparing sequential forecasters*) and Henzi & Ziegel (*Valid
    18	sequential inference on probability forecast performance*). These confidence sequences and
    19	e-processes are martingale-based. They need no i.i.d. or Markov assumption because they test
    20	`sum_t E[delta_t | F_{t-1}] <= 0`, not a marginal mean.
    21	
    22	The M4 failure ("strong serial dependence inflates promotion") is therefore partly a question of
    23	**which null is being controlled**. Any family-A mechanism that adds a Markov model must say why
    24	the conditional-mean null is the wrong target for false promotion. If it is the right target,
    25	family A is solving a problem those papers have already removed. Under a Markov null, validity
    26	becomes model-dependent, which is a regression in assumptions. Both papers should be required
    27	comparators, ported to IPW-observed paired losses.
    28	
    29	---
    30	
    31	## (a) Gap-indexed Markov likelihood / running-MLE universal-inference e-processes (A1, A4, A5, D5)
    32	
    33	**Nearest prior art (verified)**
    34	- Wasserman, Ramdas, Balakrishnan, *Universal inference*, PNAS 117(29):16880-16890, 2020.
    35	  DOI 10.1073/pnas.1922664117; arXiv:1912.11436. Introduces the split LRT and running-MLE e-values
    36	  for composite nulls, which only need the null MLE to be computable.
    37	- Ramdas, Ruf, Larsson, Koolen, *Testing exchangeability: fork-convexity, supermartingales, and
    38	  e-processes*, Int. J. Approximate Reasoning (accepted); arXiv:2102.00630. A binary-sequence
    39	  e-process built from universal inference and mixtures against **Markovian alternatives** of any
    40	  order. It is an e-process but not a supermartingale.
    41	- Shekhar, *Optimal Anytime-Valid Tests for Composite Nulls*, arXiv:2512.20039, Dec 2025. Shows that
    42	  a universal predictor divided by the running null MLE is an optimal e-process (finite alphabet,
    43	  i.i.d.).
    44	- Saha & Ramdas, *Non-partitioned e-detectors for nonparametric sequential change detection*,
    45	  arXiv:2607.28322, Jul 2026. Takes an infimum over null distributions of point-null e-processes,
    46	  with changes in Markov transition matrices as an explicit example. The repo's prior read notes a
    47	  Markov(p,q) likelihood e-process valid on the full chain without thinning (Prop A.1; partial read).
    48	- Kalbfleisch & Lawless, *The analysis of panel data under a Markov assumption*, JASA
    49	  80(392):863-871, 1985. DOI 10.1080/01621459.1985.10478195. This is the classical gap-indexed
    50	  likelihood: a chain observed only at irregular panel times, with the transition probability over
    51	  each gap.
    52	
    53	**What is known.** Running-MLE likelihood-ratio e-processes for composite nulls are standard. They
    54	have been instantiated for Markov alternatives (Ramdas et al.) and Markov change detection (Saha &
    55	Ramdas). Likelihoods for Markov chains observed at irregular times (`P^gap`, or `exp(Q*gap)`) are
    56	40 years old.
    57	
    58	**What would be new.** We found no paper combining (i) a composite **null** of first-order binary
    59	Markov loss chains with unknown persistence, (ii) **observation times chosen by the tester's own
    60	predictable query policy**, scored with gap-indexed transition likelihoods, and (iii) a paired
    61	incumbent/challenger promotion decision. The searches covered arXiv, PMLR, Annals/JRSSB records
    62	and an extended web search. The defensible new element is narrow. It is a lemma that the
    63	gap-indexed running-MLE ratio stays an e-process when gaps are predictable, so the missingness is
    64	ignorable for the likelihood because query decisions depend only on observed history. It also
    65	needs a statement of what this buys over thinning.
    66	
    67	**Substantive or renaming?** It is borderline. The validity argument is close to a plug-in of
    68	universal inference into an ignorable-missingness observed-data likelihood. It is substantive only
    69	if one of two things holds. Either (1) the gap-aware e-process has a provable or measured power or
    70	latency gain over thinned and conditional-mean alternatives at matched budget, or (2) the Markov
    71	null is argued to be the correct false-promotion target (see the cross-cutting finding).
    72	Otherwise it is a modelling choice that adds a validity assumption: first-order, stationary,
    73	correctly specified.
    74	
    75	**Ablation that would show it matters.** Use the same query schedule (predictable, irregular gaps)
    76	and compare four variants: (i) gap-aware Markov e-process, (ii) gap-ignorant Markov (treats
    77	observed labels as consecutive), (iii) i.i.d. betting e-process, and (iv) Choe-Ramdas
    78	conditional-mean e-process. Report false-promotion rate on rho in {0, .5, .8, .95} nulls,
    79	including misspecified dependence (order-2 AR, regime switching), and promotion latency on
    80	F0_better. Variant (ii) shows that the *gap* part matters. Misspecification shows the cost of the
    81	model.
    82	
    83	**Hardest competitor.** Choe & Ramdas (and Henzi & Ziegel) on the conditional-mean null: valid
    84	under arbitrary dependence with no model. On the model-based side, the Saha-Ramdas Markov
    85	likelihood e-process.
    86	
    87	## (b) Dependence-robust sequential tests: block/thinned e-values, batch martingales, mixing-based CS (A1, A4 alternatives)
    88	
    89	**Nearest prior art (verified)**
    90	- Henzi & Ziegel, *Valid sequential inference on probability forecast performance*, Biometrika
    91	  109(3):647-663, 2022; arXiv:2103.08402. Erratum in Biometrika 109(4):1181-1182: the original
    92	  anytime-valid construction holds only for lag h=1, and a corrected stopping-time construction is
    93	  given for h>1. Lag-h dependence is handled by restructuring the sequence, which is the closest
    94	  verified "thinning" precedent.
    95	- Choe & Ramdas, *Comparing sequential forecasters*, Operations Research 72(4):1368-1387, 2024. DOI
    96	  10.1287/opre.2021.0792; arXiv:2110.00115. Distribution-free CS and e-processes for a time-varying
    97	  forecaster difference.
    98	- Waudby-Smith, Arbour, Sinha, Kennedy, Ramdas, *Time-uniform central limit theory and asymptotic
    99	  confidence sequences*, Ann. Statist. 52(6):2613-2640, 2024. DOI 10.1214/24-AOS2408;
   100	  arXiv:2103.06476. Asymptotic CS via strong approximation.
   101	- Howard, Ramdas, McAuliffe, Sekhon, *Time-uniform, nonparametric, nonasymptotic confidence
   102	  sequences*, Ann. Statist. 49(2):1055-1080, 2021. DOI 10.1214/20-AOS1991; arXiv:1810.08240.
   103	- A blocking-based CS under mixing noise appears in *Linear Bandits with Non-i.i.d. Noise*,
   104	  arXiv:2505.20017. **UNVERIFIED:** authors and venue were not confirmed; only the title and
   105	  blocking idea were seen in search snippets.
   106	- No time-uniform (anytime-valid) CS for Markov-chain means indexed by mixing time was found.
   107	  Fixed-n Markov concentration exists but was not audited here.
   108	
   109	**What is known.** Thinning or lag-splitting to restore martingale structure, and martingale CS
   110	that need no independence, are both established.
   111	
   112	**What would be new.** Little. A mixing-time-calibrated block or thinning e-value for paired
   113	IPW-observed losses under a label budget could be a lemma, but the conditional-mean route already
   114	gives validity without mixing assumptions.
   115	
   116	**Substantive or renaming?** It is mostly tuning (block length, thinning stride). It counts as a
   117	baseline arm, not a contribution.
   118	
   119	**Ablation.** Compare block length or stride in {1, 5, 20, 1/(1-rho)} against conditional-mean
   120	e-processes, measuring the validity/latency frontier.
   121	
   122	**Hardest competitor.** Choe & Ramdas.
   123	
   124	## (c) Active / adaptive query density under autocorrelation (A2, C3, C4)
   125	
   126	**Nearest prior art (verified)**
   127	- Chernoff, *Sequential design of experiments*, Ann. Math. Statist., 1959. DOI
   128	  10.1214/aoms/1177706205. **UNVERIFIED:** page range.
   129	- Naghshvar & Javidi, *Active sequential hypothesis testing*, Ann. Statist. 41(6):2703-2738, 2013.
   130	  DOI 10.1214/13-AOS1144; arXiv:1203.4626.
   131	- Reynolds, Amin, Arnold, Nachlas, *X-bar charts with variable sampling intervals*, Technometrics
   132	  30(2):181-192, 1988. DOI 10.1080/00401706.1988.10488366. Adapts the sampling interval to the last
   133	  observation, a direct precedent for adaptive evidence density in monitoring.
   134	- Banerjee & Veeravalli, *Data-efficient quickest change detection in minimax settings*,
   135	  arXiv:1211.3729 (submitted to IEEE TIT; journal reference not shown on the arXiv page). Bayesian
   136	  precursor: arXiv:1105.1361. Uses on/off observation control under an observation-cost
   137	  constraint.
   138	- Xu, Karampatziakis, Mineiro, *Active, anytime-valid risk controlling prediction sets*, NeurIPS
   139	  2024; arXiv:2406.10490. Budgeted optimal labelling policy `q* ∝ sqrt(E[(R-rhat)^2|x])`.
   140	- Zrnic & Candès, *Active statistical inference*, ICML 2024, PMLR 235:62993-63010;
   141	  arXiv:2403.03208.
   142	- Kossen, Farquhar, Gal, Rainforth, *Active testing: sample-efficient model evaluation*, ICML 2021,
   143	  PMLR 139:5753-5763; arXiv:2103.05331.
   144	- Li, Kadambi, Saidi, Ramamurthy, Dasarathy, Berisha, *Active sequential two-sample testing*, TMLR
   145	  2024; arXiv:2301.12616.
   146	
   147	**What is known.** Variance-optimal label allocation (i.i.d.), on/off sampling for change
   148	detection, and adaptive sampling intervals in SPC are all established.
   149	
   150	**What would be new.** We found no paper choosing the label **spacing** for an anytime-valid test
   151	to maximise information per label when consecutive outcomes are positively autocorrelated. Under
   152	persistence rho, a label taken right after another carries roughly `(1-rho^2)`-discounted
   153	information. The defensible element is a value-of-information rule per label that is a function of
   154	the estimated persistence and the time since the last query, and that stays predictable.
   155	
   156	**Substantive or renaming?** It is substantive if it comes with a closed-form or measured
   157	information-per-label gain over uniform pacing and over Xu's q*. If the rule is a tuned schedule
   158	("query less often after a query"), it is a VSI-chart heuristic.
   159	
   160	**Ablation.** At matched B=200, compare uniform pacing, Xu q*, a front-loaded schedule, and the
   161	autocorrelation-aware spacing, across rho. Report labels-to-promotion and false promotion. Add a
   162	spacing-oracle row to bound the attainable gain.
   163	
   164	**Hardest competitor.** Xu et al. q* (combined with a conditional-mean e-process). On change
   165	detection, DE-CuSum.
   166	
   167	## (d) Restarts, e-detectors, monitoring reserve, certified rollback, multi-timescale (B1-B3, B5, B6, D3, D4)
   168	
   169	**Nearest prior art (verified)**
   170	- Shin, Ramdas, Rinaldo, *E-detectors: a nonparametric framework for sequential change detection*,
   171	  arXiv:2203.03532 (reported published in New England J. Statist. Data Sci., 2023). Covers
   172	  e-SR/e-CUSUM and mixtures for statistical and computational efficiency.
   173	- Saha & Ramdas, arXiv:2607.28322 (see (a)), and Saha & Ramdas, *Huber-robust likelihood ratio
   174	  tests for composite nulls and alternatives*, arXiv:2408.14015 (listed as accepted, IEEE TIT).
   175	- Shekhar & Ramdas, *Sequential changepoint detection via backward confidence sequences*, ICML
   176	  2023, PMLR 202:30908-30930; arXiv:2302.02544.
   177	- Banerjee & Veeravalli, data-efficient QCD (see (c)): sampling control with change detection.
   178	- Podkopaev & Ramdas, *Tracking the risk of a deployed model and detecting harmful distribution
   179	  shifts*, ICLR 2022; arXiv:2110.06177.
   180	
   181	**What is known.** Restartable SR/CUSUM-type e-detectors, mixtures over start times and scales,
   182	harmful-shift monitoring, and observation-cost-constrained change detection are all known.
   183	
   184	**What would be new.** The candidate is a joint budget split between pre-promotion certification
   185	and post-promotion detection (reserve), with a rollback gate that has its own error accounting. No
   186	paper we found couples a hard label budget with both a promotion e-process and a post-promotion
   187	e-detector. Critically, the guarantees differ: ARL or false-alarm probability for detection versus
   188	P(ever falsely promote) for the gate. The claim must state which error each component controls and
   189	how they combine (union bound or alpha-splitting).
   190	
   191	**Substantive or renaming?** It is mostly composition and budget tuning. The reserve fraction
   192	{10, 15, 25}% is a hyperparameter. Multi-timescale restarts are an e-detector mixture. The one
   193	substantive piece is a correct joint error statement for promote-then-rollback cycles with
   194	switching cost, if it is proved and not only simulated.
   195	
   196	**Ablation.** On reversal and recurrence regimes, compare reserve 0% vs 10/15/25%, rollback on vs
   197	off, single-scale vs multi-scale. Report regret vs oracle, number of switches, and empirical
   198	P(any false promotion or false rollback). Also run on the null to show that the reserve costs
   199	latency there.
   200	
   201	**Hardest competitor.** A gated host plus a Shin-Ramdas-Rinaldo e-detector restart, with a fixed
   202	budget split. This is a two-line composition of published parts that the candidate must beat.
   203	
   204	## (e) Budget shadow pricing, pending-label credit, deadline-aware acquisition (C1, C2, C5, C6, B4)
   205	
   206	**Nearest prior art (verified)**
   207	- Badanidiyuru, Kleinberg, Slivkins, *Bandits with knapsacks*, FOCS 2013. DOI 10.1109/FOCS.2013.30;
   208	  arXiv:1305.2545. Lagrangian/primal-dual resource pricing. **UNVERIFIED:** journal version.
   209	- Xu et al. 2024 and Zrnic & Candès 2024 (see (c)): budget-constrained labelling policies with an
   210	  expected-rate (not hard) budget.
   211	- Joulani, György, Szepesvári, *Online learning under delayed feedback*, ICML 2013, PMLR
   212	  28:1453-1461; arXiv:1306.0686.
   213	- Vernade, Cappé, Perchet, *Stochastic bandit models for delayed conversions*, UAI 2017, PMLR
   214	  R15:730-739; arXiv:1706.09186.
   215	- Desautels, Krause, Burdick, *Parallelizing exploration-exploitation tradeoffs in Gaussian process
   216	  bandit optimization*, JMLR 15:4053-4103, 2014; arXiv:1206.6402. "Hallucinated" observations for
   217	  pending queries, the closest precedent for pending-label credit.
   218	- Lindon & Kallus, *Design-based anytime-valid inference for randomized experiments with delayed
   219	  outcomes and staggered entry*, ICML 2026, PMLR 306:75193-75210. An event-time filtration for
   220	  delayed outcomes.
   221	- Deadline-aware label acquisition for sequential tests: no specific prior art found.
   222	
   223	**What is known.** Dual pricing of a budget constraint, delay-robust bandits, crediting pending
   224	feedback when choosing new queries, and filtrations for delayed outcomes are all known.
   225	
   226	**What would be new.** A **hard**-budget acquisition rule for an anytime-valid test whose query
   227	probability is a predictable function of (i) a shadow price from remaining budget and horizon,
   228	(ii) information already in flight, and (iii) whether a label can arrive before the decision
   229	deadline. It must keep the e-process valid: q_t is F_{t-1}-measurable and the IPW range is
   230	declared. The REPORT identifies the hard-budget acquisition layer as unspecified in every
   231	certified method (M1), and that is the real gap.
   232	
   233	**Substantive or renaming?** Each piece alone is a rename (BwK dual, GP-BUCB hallucination). The
   234	substantive claim is the M1 latency result: certificates arrive about 1/q late under uniform
   235	pacing. A scheduler that provably or measurably closes part of that gap, at matched budget and with
   236	validity intact, would be a contribution to the *acquisition layer*, not to testing.
   237	
   238	**Ablation.** Starting from the gated host with uniform pacing, add C2 shadow price alone, C1
   239	pending credit alone, B4 deadline alone, and then all three, at B in {100, 200, 400}. Measure
   240	promotion latency on F0_better, labels wasted (pending duplicates, post-deadline arrivals), and
   241	null false promotion. The REPORT's convergence at B=1000 is a built-in negative control.
   242	
   243	**Hardest competitor.** Xu et al. q* with a hard-budget cap, plus a front-loaded schedule
   244	("spend early"), which is trivial to implement.
   245	
   246	## (f) Certification gates on fast online model selectors (D1, D2, D4, D6)
   247	
   248	**Nearest prior art (verified)**
   249	- Karimi, Gürel, Karlaš, Rausch, Zhang, Krause, *Online active model selection for pre-trained
   250	  classifiers* (Model Picker), AISTATS 2021, PMLR 130:307-315; arXiv:2010.09818.
   251	- Liu, Xia, Stevens, Chen, *Contextual active model selection* (CAMS), NeurIPS 2024; arXiv:2207.06030.
   252	- Okanovic, Kirsch, Kasper, Hoefler, Krause, Gürel, *All models are wrong, some are useful: model
   253	  selection with limited labels* (Model Selector), AISTATS 2025, PMLR 258:2035-2043;
   254	  arXiv:2410.13609.
   255	- Karampatziakis, Mineiro, Ramdas, *Off-policy confidence sequences*, ICML 2021, PMLR
   256	  139:5301-5310; arXiv:2102.09540. Includes a **gated deployment** application.
   257	- Johari, Koomen, Pekelis, Walsh, *Always valid inference: continuous monitoring of A/B tests*,
   258	  Operations Research 70(3):1806-1821, 2022. DOI 10.1287/opre.2021.2135; arXiv:1512.04922.
   259	- Howard et al. 2021 (see (b)); Lindon & Malek, *Anytime-valid inference for multinomial count
   260	  data*, NeurIPS 2022; arXiv:2011.03567 (includes canary testing); Lindon & Kallus 2026 (see (e)).
   261	- Digalakis, Pérignon, Saurin, Sentenac, *The Challenger: when do new data sources justify
   262	  switching machine learning models?*, arXiv:2512.18390.
   263	
   264	**What is known.** Active selectors give regret or PAC identification, not anytime false-promotion
   265	control. Anytime-valid gating of a candidate policy (Karampatziakis et al.) and always-valid A/B or
   266	canary testing are standard.
   267	
   268	**What would be new.** "The selector proposes, an anytime certificate disposes": the gate reuses
   269	the selector's own (predictable) query probabilities as IPW weights, so certification costs no
   270	extra labels. The defensible element is the measured M2 finding: all eight uncertified families
   271	falsely promote on 64-100% of null streams. The other part is a host-agnostic wrapper (D6) whose
   272	validity follows from predictability of the host's query rule.
   273	
   274	**Substantive or renaming?** The method is composition. Validity comes from the gate, and the
   275	gate is published. It is substantive as a **benchmark/negative finding** and as a transfer result
   276	(D6) only if the wrapper keeps most of the selector's speed. Otherwise it is "gated deployment with
   277	a different proposal".
   278	
   279	**Ablation.** For each host, compare host alone, host plus gate (shared labels), host plus gate
   280	(separate gate labels), and the gate alone with uniform proposal. Report false-promotion rate,
   281	switches, and latency. If gate-alone matches host-plus-gate, the selector contributes nothing.
   282	
   283	**Hardest competitor.** Karampatziakis et al. gated deployment with the stronger `wmax_t = 1/q_t`
   284	re-port (already in the REPORT).
   285	
   286	## (g) Closest certified competitors overall
   287	
   288	Verified:
   289	- Karampatziakis et al. 2021 (above).
   290	- Waudby-Smith, Wu, Ramdas, Karampatziakis, Mineiro, *Anytime-valid off-policy inference for
   291	  contextual bandits*, arXiv:2210.10768. No known bound on importance weights is needed. This is
   292	  directly relevant to the declared-HT-range cap the REPORT blames for part of M1.
   293	- Balachandran, *Pay only for disagreement: certified no-regression verdicts for model updates*
   294	  (DISCERN), arXiv:2609.17560, Jul 2026 (v1, single author, not peer-reviewed as far as seen). The
   295	  REPORT records a `/4` omission in our port's `psi_E`, which is now fixed.
   296	- Xu, Karampatziakis, Mineiro 2024 (above).
   297	- Arnold, Gavrilopoulos, Schulz, Ziegel, *Sequential model confidence sets*, arXiv:2404.18678;
   298	  reported in JRSSB 2026, DOI 10.1093/jrsssb/qkag066. Author list and version varied across
   299	  records, so cite the arXiv record.
   300	- Choe & Ramdas 2024; Henzi & Ziegel 2022; Podkopaev & Ramdas 2022; Chugg, Cortes-Gomez, Wilder,
   301	  Ramdas, *Auditing fairness by betting*, NeurIPS 2023, arXiv:2305.17570 (betting audit with a
   302	  probabilistic data-collection policy). Waudby-Smith & Ramdas, *Estimating means of bounded random
   303	  variables by betting*, JRSSB 2024, DOI 10.1093/jrsssb/qkad009; arXiv:2010.09686.
   304	- Prabhu, Bhashyam, Gopalan, Sundaresan, *Sequential multi-hypothesis testing in multi-armed bandit
   305	  problems: an approach for asymptotic optimality*, IEEE TIT 68(7):4790-4817, 2022; DOI
   306	  10.1109/TIT.2022.3159600; arXiv:2007.12961. Covers switching cost plus a false-detection
   307	  constraint, a close formal analogue of "switching cost + false promotion".
   308	
   309	**Recommendation.** Add Choe-Ramdas (conditional-mean paired e-process, IPW-adapted) and
   310	Waudby-Smith et al. 2022 (no weight-bound requirement) as required certified comparators. They
   311	target M4 and the HT-range part of M1 respectively, and neither appears in the REPORT's competitor
   312	list.
   313	
   314	---
   315	
   316	## Summary table
   317	
   318	| mechanism | nearest prior art | smallest defensible novel element | substantive? | hardest competitor | required ablation |
   319	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
   320	| (a) gap-indexed Markov running-MLE e-process | Wasserman+ 2020; Ramdas+ 2102.00630; Saha-Ramdas 2607.28322; Kalbfleisch-Lawless 1985 | validity lemma for running-MLE e-process under a Markov composite null with **predictable, policy-chosen gaps** in a paired promotion test | borderline; it adds a model assumption, and is substantive only with a gain over the conditional-mean null | Choe-Ramdas conditional-mean e-process (model-free) | gap-aware vs gap-ignorant vs i.i.d. vs Choe-Ramdas, same schedule, rho grid plus misspecified dependence |
   321	| (b) block/thinned/mixing CS | Henzi-Ziegel 2022 (+erratum); Choe-Ramdas 2024; Waudby-Smith+ 2024 | none beyond a stride lemma | no (tuning) | Choe-Ramdas | stride/block grid vs conditional-mean e-process |
   322	| (c) autocorrelation-aware query density | Chernoff 1959; Naghshvar-Javidi 2013; VSI charts 1988; DE-CuSum; Xu+ 2024 | info-per-label spacing rule as a function of persistence and time since last query, predictable | yes if gain is shown or derived; otherwise VSI heuristic | Xu+ q* | uniform / Xu q* / front-load / spacing / oracle at B=200 across rho |
