    57	| A1b | 0/300 (≤.013) | 0/300 (≤.013) | 2/300 (≤.024) |
    58	| A5b | 0/300 (≤.013) | 0/300 (≤.013) | 3/300 (≤.029) |
    59	| A6b | 0/300 (≤.013) | 0/300 (≤.013) | 3/300 (≤.029) |
    60	| QSG | 78/300 (.26) | 195/300 (.65) | 6/300 (.02) |
    61	
    62	All four SUI variants sit well below .0936. This is empirical frequency on the declared
    63	model. The mathematical guarantee is the SUI argument below, and it holds only inside the
    64	declared class.
    65	
    66	## Ablations (20 dev seeds)
    67	
    68	* A1-nogap: same SUI with a constant transition (gap ignored) has null promotion **.40 (r8) and
    69	  .60 (r95)**, so the gap indexing is what makes A1 valid.
    70	* A1-rho8 (declared rho <= .8) has null r95 promotion 1.00. A declared bound fails as soon as it is
    71	  violated, and it buys no iid power (F0_better identical to A1).
    72	* A1-nostart: F0_better +1.9, F6 -1.2. Starts matter little here, and A1 and A1-nostart both fail
    73	  F1_late_800.
    74	* A2-fixedgap6 vs A2: F0_better +19.7, bursty +50.8. Adaptive density beats fixed thinning, but
    75	  neither is safe with iid evidence.
    76	* A5-fixedgap6 vs A5: F0_better +25.9.
    77	* A3-noinflight vs A3, and A6-noinflight vs A6: within ±2.5 everywhere. The dual-clock mechanism
    78	  has no material effect.
    79	
    80	## Diagnostics behind the negative parts
    81	
    82	For SUI evidence after 100 queried outcomes (10 synthetic sequences, scratch probe), with the
    83	oracle alternative likelihood minus the iid p=.5 likelihood as the benchmark:
    84	* iid p=.65 alternative: oracle 4.4 nats. The free-rho null absorbs .4, the cell upper bound adds
    85	  .6-1.6 depending on grid resolution (301 rho x 121 p cells used), and the numerator mixture costs 1.6.
    86	* p=.65, rho=.8 alternative: oracle 20.5 nats, of which the free-rho null absorbs 18.5. A rho-free
    87	  test is information-limited here, so the dependent-alternative loss reflects that limit and is
    88	  not a tuning defect. Methods that "win" there (QSG, gated+front, xu) are the same methods that
    89	  falsely promote on the rho=.95 null.
    90	
    91	## Validity argument (A1/A5/A6)
    92	
    93	Filtration F_k: queried outcomes z_1..z_k in issue order with their issue events, the disagreement
    94	indicators, and private randomness. The issue time of query k+1 depends only on released labels
    95	(a subset of F_k), disagreement indicators and private randomness, so it is predictable, and the
    96	sign at that event is not read before issue. Declared null H+: the sign is a stationary two-state
    97	Markov chain in event time with P(s=1) = p <= 1/2, any persistence rho in [0,1], independent of
    98	disagreements. Then P(z_{k+1}=1 | F_k) = a z_k + (1-a)p with a = rho^{g_{k+1}}. For each start j,
    99	U_{j,k} >= sup over H+ of L(z_j..z_k | z_{j-1}, gaps). This holds because each factor's cell maximum
   100	is at a monotone corner, the product of the per-factor maxima bounds the cell sup, and the max over
   101	cells covers [0,1/2]x[0,1] (unit-tested against exact likelihoods on a coarse grid). So
   102	Q_{j,k}/U_{j,k} <= Q_{j,k}/L_theta(j..k), a mean-one nonnegative martingale from j under the true
   103	theta. The started mixture plus the tail weight of unstarted terms is a test supermartingale, so
   104	by Ville the promote test crosses 2^c/alpha with probability <= alpha/2^c in cycle c. Summed
   105	over cycles, P(any false promotion) <= alpha for every member of H+ simultaneously. No rho bound
   106	is declared.
   107	
   108	Limits:
   109	* The guarantee needs a stationary first-order Markov sign process independent of disagreements.
   110	  QSG's iid H+ covers time-varying p_t <= 1/2; A1's class does not. Higher-order or
   111	  nonstationary dependence is not covered.
   112	* The constant delay makes release order equal issue order. Out-of-order release is not handled.
   113	* Rollback tests (alpha_r) are not safety claims.
   114	
   115	## Continue / pivot / stop
   116	
   117	* **Continue A5b (with A1b as its density-off control) to 100-seed validation.** It is the only
   118	  family-A line that is valid at rho=.8 and .95 and not slower than the best certified published
   119	  method on stationary alternatives. Its advantage there is small and not significant on 20 seeds
   120	  (-5.7, CI includes 0), it loses on bursty (+22), and it cannot detect dependent alternatives.
   121	  The success criterion from the Atlas audit (CI < 0 on >= 4/6 stationary alternatives) looks
   122	  unlikely to be met. The plausible claim is "matches the best certified cost while being the only
   123	  one valid at rho=.95", and validation should test exactly that.
   124	* Stop A2, A3, A4 and A6 as separate candidates.

