    43	| B5 | +3.4 [-2.0,+8.9] | -16.0 [-26.2,-5.8] | -6.7 [-12.2,-1.2] | +7.1 [-0.5,+14.6] | +12.5 [+2.3,+22.7] | +15.0 | -7.2 |
    44	| B6 | +4.7 [-0.8,+10.1] | -11.1 [-22.2,-0.0] | -4.9 [-11.2,+1.4] | -0.0 [-5.8,+5.7] | +17.4 | +16.8 | -5.9 |
    45	| B5-reserve (=B2+B4) | -0.8 [-3.2,+1.6] | -9.7 [-15.1,-4.2] | -4.2 [-8.8,+0.4] | +0.5 [-2.3,+3.3] | +18.8 | +17.5 | -11.4 |
    46	| B5-rollback (=B1+B4) | +3.4 | -8.5 [-19.1,+2.2] | -6.9 | +7.1 | +20.0 | +14.8 | -7.2 |
    47	| B5-deadline (=B1+B2) | +3.3 | -17.4 [-28.9,-5.9] | -7.1 | +7.9 | +11.1 | +14.7 | -7.3 |
    48	
    49	Best certified on reversal/recurrent regimes is `discern_design_floor` (it rarely promotes);
    50	`CONTROL_never_switch` is cheaper still on every change regime screened. Transient: B4 and
    51	B2+B4 are worse than host by +3.6 [+0.3,+6.9]; others are ns.
    52	
    53	Path diagnostics (`fam_b.py diagnose`, replay verified identical to screen nets): on
    54	F3_reversal_800, correct rollbacks per run host .75, B2 .90, B2+B4 .95, B5 .60; mean events from
    55	reversal to rollback (censored at T when none) host 785, B2 614, B2+B4 507, B5 538. False
    56	rollbacks 0 for every candidate on stationary alternatives.
    57	
    58	Stationary price (net minus never-switch on F0_null; mostly label spend): host +10.0, B2 +10.0,
    59	B4 +9.4, B1 +7.5 to +9.0, B5/B6 +8.4. Dynamic regret vs expected-cost oracle (reversal family):
    60	host 82.8, B2 74.6, B2+B4 73.1, B5 66.8 (see `key_numbers.json`).
    61	
    62	## Interpretation
    63	
    64	* Rollback speed is capped by the label rate: ~1 queried disagreement per 10 events at B=200 and
    65	  ~0.08 nats per label, so even B2's cheaper rollback threshold needs ~500 events after a reversal.
    66	  B2 recovers ~170 events of that; it cannot recover the cost of a first promotion that typically
    67	  arrives at t~550 (the host promotes late, so there is little gain to protect before t=800).
    68	* The reserve (B1, and B5/B6 through it) "helps" reversal mainly by suppressing promotion
    69	  (promotion .70 vs 1.0 on F3), which is why it costs on late and stationary improvement. The
    70	  mechanism ablations confirm this: removing the reserve from B5 halves the reversal gain and
    71	  removes the stationary/late cost.
    72	* Multiscale restarts (B3) and deadline-aware acquisition (B4) do not move reversal or recurrent
    73	  costs; B4's useful-window reallocation is ~8% of the horizon at T=2000, delay=100.
    74	* No B candidate beats the best certified published comparator on reversal or recurrent regimes;
    75	  all inherit the host's late-change advantage (-6 to -11 vs best certified).
    76	
    77	## Disposition
    78	
    79	Continue (scoped): **B2** as the rollback component of the RMM line: zero cost before
    80	promotion, significant reversal gain over the host, no false rollbacks seen, same null rates as
    81	host. Second: **B2+B4** (`B5-reserve`) only as a variant to re-check; its transient cost is
    82	significant. Pivot: pair B2 with a host that promotes earlier (front-loaded Gated) so there is a
    83	gain to protect, and address the rho=.8 failure separately. Stop: B1, B3, B4, B5, B6 as proposed.
    84	
    85	Limitations: 20 seeds per regime; best-certified selection per regime on the same seeds (favours
    86	the comparator); the B5/B6 reserve level (.15) was fixed before screening; runtime mean ~0.2 s,
    87	max 1.34 s per episode.

--- docs/sota_sprint/family_c/README.md ---
     1	# Family C: label acquisition and economics (SOTA sprint, Agent C)
     2	
     3	Date: 2026-11-03. Code: `scripts/sota_sprint/fam_c.py` (sha256 prefix `c598771b471989b0`; behaviour
     4	identical to the screened version, later edits added the `report` CLI and fixed import order).
     5	Tests: `uv run pytest -q tests/sota_sprint/test_fam_c.py` (34 passed). Branch
     6	`research/sota-sprint-24`, uncommitted. Contract: T=2000, B=200, c_q=.05, c_s=5, alpha=.05.
     7	
     8	**Evidence class.** All numbers below are development screening diagnostics (20 seeds, default
     9	dev seed base 2026110501, the same seeds used for tuning, so tuned rows are in-sample), plus a
    10	20-seed descriptive check and a 300-seed null check at seed base 2026110513. None of this is
    11	confirmatory evidence.
    12	
    13	## What was built
    14	
    15	Every candidate changes only acquisition; the host CS, stopping rule and deployment rule are
    16	untouched. A single `Controller` produces `(pi, q)` per event: the event is recorded in the host CS
    17	with predictable probability `pi`, and if it is a recorded disagreement its label is requested with
    18	propensity `q`, which is frozen at issue. The host's importance weight is `1/q` from
    19	`ReleasedFeedback.query_probability`. On gated, `wmax_t = max(1/q, 2)` is also fixed at issue.
    20	Re-implementations of the host pacers (`Cchk:*`) reproduce `gated_adaptive_range`,
    21	`gated_adaptive_range+front` and `discern_design_floor` exactly (unit test).
    22	
    23	| id | implementation | mechanism |
    24	|---|---|---|
    25	| C1 | `GatedC` + `Controller(base="front", credit=True)` | skip (unrecorded) when released + pending labels reach `kappa * n_needed`, extrapolated from the current CS half-width and the point estimate |
    26	| C2 | `GatedC` + `Controller(base="front", shadow=True)` | query iff value per label `max(0, G_eff (T-t) - c_s) / n_remaining` >= shadow price `c_q * max(1, spend_rate (T-t)/remaining)^gamma`; `G_eff = (1-zeta) max(G_hat,0) + zeta g_hi` |
    27	| C3 | `QSGTwoStage` | QSG with concentrated phase (q=1 while undecided, at most `f B` labels and only before `tau T`), then uniform protected monitoring |
    28	| C4 | `DiscernC` + `Controller(base="verdict")` | corrected DISCERN (design floor .25): pace the remaining budget to the projected verdict time rather than T |
    29	| C5 | C1 + C2 | pending labels are credited in C2's `n_remaining` and in the C1 skip |
    30	| C6 | `GatedC`/`DiscernC` + `Controller(shadow, deadline, two_stage)` | C2 price, C3-style two-stage split (`f2 B`, then uniform thinning), deadline-aware value: label worth 0 if `(T - t - delay - collection time) G_eff <= c_s` |
    31	| tuned pacing | `gated_adaptive_range+pace_tuned`, `discern_design_floor+pace_tuned` | k-pacer `q = k rem/(rate (T-t))` or front-fraction grid incl. host (uniform) and `+front` |
    32	
    33	Skip action, declared per host: on gated (adaptive range) a skipped event is *unrecorded*, because
    34	a recorded low-q event sets `wmax = 1/q`. On DISCERN (fixed range `c = 1/pi_min`) a skipped event is
    35	recorded with `q = pi_min`. The ablations show the opposite choice is worse on both hosts:
    36	C2-floorskip is +5.8 vs C2, and C6@discern-unrecorded is ±0.3 vs C6@discern.
    37	
    38	## Validity, per candidate
    39	
    40	* **C1, C2, C5, C6 (gated MOPE host).** `q` is predictable: it uses released labels, the pending
    41	  count, budget, t and the predictions of event t, never the label of event t. `wmax_t = 1/q_t`
    42	  holds exactly, so every increment satisfies the Lemma 1 range. Unrecorded events are chosen by a
    43	  predictable coin, which is optional skipping of the hedged wealth process. Under the pointwise
    44	  promotion null `E[l0-l1 | F_{t-1}, preds_t] <= 0`, each recorded factor has conditional mean
    45	  <= 1, so Ville applies at level alpha. Caveat: under non-stationary alternatives the CS averages
    46	  over *recorded* events, not all events.
    47	* **C4 and C6@discern (PrPl-EB, `c = 4`).** `q` is clamped to `>= pi_min = .25`, so
    48	  `|Z| <= c`. `q` is predictable, and the WSR theorem needs only predictable lambda and bounded
    49	  increments.
    50	* **C3 (QSG).** Evidence is query-conditional with weight one, so any predictable `q` is valid for
    51	  the QCE null. This is unchanged from QSG.
    52	* **Not covered.** The F5 Markov-dependence null is outside every one of these nulls. Its rates
    53	  below are empirical only.
    54	
