   111	
   112	## Dispositions
   113	
   114	* C1: SCREENED_NEGATIVE. At the tuned kappa it is identical to `+front`, and the aggressive
   115	  setting is harmful. Pending labels rarely settle a gated verdict: the betting CS narrows slower
   116	  than the 1/sqrt(n) extrapolation.
   117	* C2: SCREENED_PROMISING (weak). It saves labels on null and hopeless streams at no false-promotion
   118	  cost, but the overall dev gain did not hold on fresh seeds, and it fails under a tight budget.
   119	  Next: make the price budget-tightness-safe (cap gamma, or set the price from B/(D T)), then
   120	  re-screen B100/B400 on fresh seeds.
   121	* C3: SCREENED_NEGATIVE. Calibration selected "no split" (f=1), which is QSG. Every protected
   122	  split costs 2 to 6.
   123	* C4: SCREENED_NEGATIVE. It ties both its host and the tuned pacing baseline. Corrected DISCERN is
   124	  limited by the event clock (lam_max) more than by labels.
   125	* C5: SCREENED_NEGATIVE. It is no better than C2 (+0.5).
   126	* C6: SCREENED_PROMISING (weak) on gated, the best gated variant on held-out seeds, though not
   127	  significantly better than +front. The transfer to DISCERN is negative (+1.9 vs host).
   128	* Neither candidate beats QSG (internal) on mean net. Against the per-regime best certified
   129	  published method, C2 wins 4 regimes (nulls, F1_late, S_delay800) and loses F3_reversal.
   130	
   131	Assessment: **pivot.** The economics in Family C mostly buys label-cost savings of about 6 to 7
   132	units per null episode. It does not repair certification latency, because the gated host is
   133	information-limited at B=200 (+front exhausts its budget before certifying in about 30% of
   134	F0_better seeds). It is still worth carrying a budget-safe C2/C6 price as an attachable
   135	label-saver on top of a stronger evidence engine (QSG/QCE). It is not a standalone contribution.
   136	
